    <contentRows>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "contentData": [
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "dd183f78-7d69-4eda-9b4c-a25970583a28",
      "key": "961cb099-7aa5-410e-b125-85d06aa83e78",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "content",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "Layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EI want to be upfront about something, what you\u2019re seeing in this video is only possible because of the custom tools I\u2019ve written to work with Umbraco AI. Without those, none of this happens.\n\nIn this video I show you how I can type a single prompt and have Umbraco AI go off, create a fully structured article, populate it with content blocks, add images, and publish it to my site. All in about 20\u201330 seconds. I find that genuinely exciting and I think you will too.\n\nHere\u2019s what I walk through:\n\n- Prompting Umbraco AI to write and publish a complete blog article from scratch\n- Watching it pick and fill content blocks automatically; text, code, tables, quotes, images\n- Asking it to split content and insert a new block mid-article\n- Getting it to add an image block at the end based on a description\n- Seeing the finished article live on the front end\n\nThis is the kind of thing that could be a real game changer for your editors, especially if you\u2019re on Umbraco 17. Even if you just want a solid starting point for an article rather than a blank page, this sort of tooling makes that really easy.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wrfwi7oGqG8\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noopener\u0022\u003ERead the original article\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
